  "query_preview": "..\nThe system that brought precipitation to the Inland Northwest \nyesterday into this (Tuesday) morning has moved out of the area, \nallowing for a short break in precipitation through this afternoon.\nA second, stronger upper-level low will move in later tonight, \nbringing a renewed chance of showers to lower elevations, \nslightly breezy winds, and mountain snow accumulations. As this \ncolder system moves in late this evening, snow levels will drop \nto around 2000 to 3000 feet. Mountain snow will start around 5pm\nthis afternoon in the Cascades and in the far northern ID \nPanhandle. The greatest snow accumulations are expected at \nStevens and Washington Passes. Probabilities for 6 inches of \nsnow through Wednesday afternoon are 70% at Stevens Pass and 45%\nat Washington Pass. Other passes, such as Sherman and Lookout \nPasses, are set to see around 2-4 inches of snow. Stevens and \nWashington Passes will see winter driving conditions, and a \nWinter Weather Advisory has been issued for these two passes \nfrom 5pm tonight through 5pm tomorrow (Wednesday). Additionally,\nsubfreezing temperatures will present a threat for outdoor \nenthusiasts. If you are planning to be in these areas over the \nnext few days, please take precautions. \n\nWhile winter impacts along mountain passes will be the most impactful\npart of this system, there is a chance of showers across \nelevations below 3000 feet alongside wind gusts in the basin \nareas. Winds will increase from the southwest as the low moves \ninland, occurring tonight (Tuesday) through tomorrow morning. \nWind gusts in the Columbia Basin, Spokane, Palouse, and West \nPlains could reach 25-30 mph. While these breezy conditions are \nexpected, widespread wind related impacts are not anticipated. \nRain is expected across many lower elevations, with the highest \ntotals expected in the Idaho Panhandle and northern mountains, \nwhich could see up to an inch of rain. Temperatures cool after \nthe system moves through, with highs in the 40s and low 50s and \nlows in the 30s.\n\nFriday through Monday: The low is expected to remain just off the\ncoast of Washington on Friday and Saturday while gradually \nweakening. Precipitation chances will decrease during this \nperiod, with any remaining showers confined to over the \nmountains due to orographic lift. Although snow levels could \nfall to around 2,000 feet across north-central Washington, \nlimited precipitation chances are expected to keep additional \nsnow accumulations minimal. By Sunday into Monday, the low is \nforecast to shift south as a ridge builds into the region, \nsupporting a gradual warming trend into Monday. Ensemble \nclusters show approximately 80% support for this pattern. /AS",
